{"title":"2015 USAMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_6\">Problem 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/e/12e2ac9969c754c95d350f444baf099c11e7a15a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;\\lambda&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"76\" height=\"13\" />, and let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> be a multiset of positive integers. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/9/a193146e791b5f63e007a243ca1b56d6dc08fe0d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n=\\{a\\in A: a\\leq n\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"172\" height=\"20\" />. Assume that for every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64508262bb2272e129bd59c31142b17e78b6ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />, the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> contains at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/e/c0e64b7126f2100c61306fcac34e3020b564444e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\lambda$\" width=\"21\" height=\"13\" />numbers. Show that there are infinitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64508262bb2272e129bd59c31142b17e78b6ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> for which the sum of the elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> is at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/e/38ed64d195bacb59a2ee995012e9f56f5f260242.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"81\" height=\"38\" />. (A multiset is a set-like collection of elements in which order is ignored, but repetition of elements is allowed and multiplicity of elements is significant. For example, multisets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/5/62511f4d99c1621775385afa593225a56e41f220.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/c/23c36089517f51f966031093d606a6787e44d4a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{2, 1, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> are equivalent, but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/7/d3711c073dfc337979caa9c692c1fb81d66ac874.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 1, 2, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/5/62511f4d99c1621775385afa593225a56e41f220.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{1, 2, 3\\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"60\" height=\"18\" /> differ.)\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_USAMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Proposed by mengmeng142857.\n</p><p>We prove this by contradiction. Suppose that the number of times that an integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> appears in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/d/cdd004089104116a03d8507acc9a3876c4037156.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"15\" />. Let the sum of the elements in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/c/1eccfbd65334353f671fb71c27b9b9f2c689b0bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"15\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/a/70a9e0d4e61c3e6c520ac2ae18d1c4e3a0e45a4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_n=\\sum_{i=1}^{n}i\\cdot k_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"109\" height=\"53\" />. If there are only finitely many <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64508262bb2272e129bd59c31142b17e78b6ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/b/50b12cb78e1b4c4c6b6f33630312e9897e71a3c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_n\\leq\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"125\" height=\"38\" />, then by the Well Ordering Principle, there must be a largest <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/8/8f8b136ddd69fa7bbaf637eef38def08ee25a10a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"50\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/1/281234fe32e1995b51b9c77706cabb4bc17b3ea7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_m\\leq\\frac{m(m+1)}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"139\" height=\"38\" />, and for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/2/eb2b326840add3c30cfa0bba450b44959d46f64e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;m$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/4/aa42bfeb7b7c61ce3f4a6904cd1983c51e553585.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_n&gt;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"128\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p><p>Now, observe that for some <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/2/eb2b326840add3c30cfa0bba450b44959d46f64e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;m$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"50\" height=\"10\" />,\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e129557c73531eb5abd78ba8bad80ec8cc01bc2a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot S_{n}+\\frac{1}{n(n-1)}\\cdot S_{n-1}+\\frac{1}{(n-1)(n-2)}\\cdot S_{n-2}+...+\\frac{1}{2\\cdot1}\\cdot S_{1} =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i \\leq n\\lambda\\]\" width=\"554\" height=\"47\" />\nAlso, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/65080911d3af8593b559f271a591e68d902e24a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{n}}{n}&gt;\\frac{n+1}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"104\" height=\"37\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/c/77c130c9156b625061302a2976eaeb3ab6c6e905.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{n-1}}{n(n-1)}&gt;\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"118\" height=\"42\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/2/7b2469fb9ed252dd9a056383aacb38110f413bf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{n-2}}{(n-1)(n-2)}&gt;\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"164\" height=\"42\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/c/bec1f1dbdd017dec876ead39704dd597b8f6cbdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\hdots$\" width=\"19\" height=\"2\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/8/cc86d80e6a1b9fea5941c31391dd967d09c3052e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{m+1}}{(m+2)(m+1)}&gt;\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"174\" height=\"42\" />, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/3/0/230c7d308a34ded5b06a0076cf70477de239b4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_{m}}{(m+1)m}\\leq \\frac{1}{2}\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"128\" height=\"42\" />.\n</p><p>Now, for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/4/a64508262bb2272e129bd59c31142b17e78b6ad3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\in\\mathbb{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"15\" />, we let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/f/2/bf2ac3f9519275033602ef981ea87001857ca763.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{S_n}{(n+1)n}-\\frac{1}{2}\\lambda=d_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"159\" height=\"42\" />, then\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/c/1cc052b6bfc642375627ba574dc9523db5ae559e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{1}{n}\\cdot S_n+\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\\frac{S_i}{i(i+1)} =n\\lambda+(n+1)\\cdot d_n+\\sum_{i=m+1}^{n-1}d_i+\\sum_{i=1}^{m}d_i \\leq n\\lambda\\]\" width=\"477\" height=\"51\" />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/e/b1ec5df309d5e7bda7f1342735ced96d8f75c7c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^{m}d_i=-c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"90\" height=\"48\" /> (where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is positive, otherwise we already have a contradiction), then \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/2/702217ae8c527e616c6055bd3fa6f30b7021d392.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[n\\lambda-c&lt; n\\lambda+(n+1)\\cdot d_n+\\sum_{i=m+1}^{n-1}d_i-c =\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i\\leq n\\lambda\\]\" width=\"434\" height=\"51\" />\nDividing both sides by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/1/151916efbd82a7b81594c0f176c6dc991b3d5fbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda-c/n&lt;\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i}{n}\\leq \\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"182\" height=\"39\" />. Taking the limit yields\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/0/67010bf780043b1bfba798de3fb8471fb5d874be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}{\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i}{n}}=\\lambda$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"135\" height=\"39\" />.\nObserve that this would imply that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/2/512dda4b216e89af382ad6169017b8a5ce307b60.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}{k_n} =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}{\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i-\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}k_i\\right)} =\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}{\\left(n\\cdot\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i}{n}-(n-1)\\cdot\\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}k_i}{n-1}\\right)} =\\lambda\\]\" width=\"625\" height=\"53\" />\nHowever, as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/2/fd26dda3f4f507f3fdfaff4fe0b468bee54e7c09.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"15\" /> is an integer, it is impossible for it to converge to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/4/ce4588fd900d02afcbd260bc07f54cce49a7dc4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lambda$\" width=\"10\" height=\"13\" />. This yields the desired contradiction.\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017035029\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.016 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.025 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 163/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 8676/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:14526:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017035029 and revision id 99481. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}